  "notes": "A&M/Interscope Oakland soloist: Ron Fair's orchestral pop-R&B laid over Bay and Atlanta beds, 2005–2010. The research gives \"70–98 straight; ballads 70–83 half-time\", so the straight band is the base with the mode at 93, a doubly-sourced tempo. Three drum deltas: \"kick on 1 and the 8th before 3\" at two or three hits a bar, \"clap+snare on 2&4\" pushed 2–6 ms with `quantizeStrength` at 0.66, and \"a live tambourine/snap layer from Fair's sessions\" — a three-lane tambourine/snap/shaker bed at two to five hits a bar with `velocityVar` at 0.24, which is what a played overdub does to a programmed grid; the hat lane stays low in the mix at 0.26 fill density. Harmony is the entry's explicit contrast with the lane: \"4-chord minor cycles with real functional motion (i–VI–VII–iv families) rather than 2-chord vamps\", so that family carries top weight and `maxChords` is 4. \"Belted alto with heavy melisma and cracked-voice ornament, very high note-density in the last chorus\" is a 4–12 density, a 0.64 step share with a real leap share, and the second-highest arch weight in this section; \"**Fair-arranged strings** entering at the bridge\" is a `sustain_pad`-led counter at +12. The low end is \"808 sub or a played bass on roots; low end restrained to leave room for the string arrangement\", so it is a bass part with no `drums.bass808` block. ⚠ The research marks the key claim `[LOW-CONF]`. ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED: the entry's Keys line gives \"minor-mode with gospel modulation [LOW-CONF]\" and enumerates no tonic, so the values here and their weights are this author's reading of that line.",
